Samuel J. Ling, Jeff Sanny and William Moebs. University Physics, Volume 1, OpenStax. [Publisher book](https://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1) · [Chapter 14: Fluid mechanics](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/14-introduction).

Read chapter 14 in full with its problems. Work the preceding mechanics chapters on vectors, acceleration, forces, work, energy and momentum when those quantities are unfamiliar. This gives a first physical account before the longer engineering and mathematical texts. Return to the named sections rather than assuming the reader remembers every earlier equation.

Use the publisher's web edition and its download choices, preserving the accessed edition and component-specific notices. The chapter is introductory; the sustained development continues in [Lectures in Elementary Fluid Dynamics — James M. McDonough](Elementary-Fluid-Dynamics-McDonough.lingua#r_7F9FTDV2VV4P28PG16J5PJSK18) and [Introdução à Mecânica dos Fluidos e aos Fenômenos de Transporte — Gobbi and colleagues](Mecanica-dos-Fluidos-e-Transportes-Gobbi-Dias.lingua#r_1F9KHVCP86X68RR7DTZASE5F1J). All source media remain external.

**Return:** [Fluid dynamics sources — books, courses, methods and physical evidence](../Sources.lingua#r_3Y3TVV9VQAB50M2ZSECK5JTYVG).

**Shared physics foundation — 31 October 2026:** begin with sections 3.1–3.4, 5.1–5.4, constant-force parts of chapters 7–8, and sections 9.1–9.3. Work the algebraic and graphical exercises using the quantities introduced in the core. Return to variable-force integrals, general vector dynamics and other unfamiliar calculus after that mathematical preparation. These assignments complement the original fluid-mechanics reading above; they do not replace it or claim that the complete physics formation is finished.

Selected publisher opening passages inspected for this block: [3.1, position and displacement](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/3-1-position-displacement-and-average-velocity), [3.4, constant acceleration](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/3-4-motion-with-constant-acceleration), [5.1, forces](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/5-1-forces), [5.3, Newton's second law](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/5-3-newtons-second-law), [7.3, work and energy](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/7-3-work-energy-theorem), [9.2, impulse](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/9-2-impulse-and-collisions), and [9.3, momentum conservation](https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/9-3-conservation-of-linear-momentum). Assigned chapters and exercise sets were not fully audited. The trail's worked numbers and investigations are original hypothetical examples, not reproduced experiments.

